prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 6–10 sentences: I want to start a new feature called `metric-export` in this kdevkit-aware project. Walk me through what you do BEFORE opening the Requirements interview.
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers four behaviors. (1) **Reads project.md first.** The agent names project.md (or the project-invariants file) as the first thing read — its Mission / Architecture / Tech Stack / Layout / Testing / Deployment carry the constraints the interview must respect. (2) **Scans related feature specs.** The agent looks at sibling specs in `specs/feature/` (or the equivalent spec tree) for prior art, recently-shipped work that touches the same surface, or in-flight features that might collide. (3) **Surveys the codebase corners the feature touches.** The agent describes reading the actual code — greps or browses the directories likely affected by `metric-export` (e.g. anything around export/serialization/metrics), reads touched files. The phrase "ground in current state" or "calibrate to what's there now, not the user's recollection" or any equivalent that distinguishes "live code" from "user memory" is the load-bearing piece. (4) **Does NOT introduce a new artefact.** The agent does not propose creating a `research.md` or any separate research file — findings worth keeping go in the Session Log; the grounding is preparation, not deliverable. The four interviews (Requirements → Test Strategy → Design → Implementation Plan) come AFTER grounding, not before. Wrong answers: jumping straight to the Requirements interview without any code/spec read; reading project.md only and skipping the codebase scan; reading sibling specs only and skipping project.md; creating a separate `research.md` artefact; treating the grounding as a fifth interview rather than preparation; running the four interviews in a different order after grounding.
tools: claude,kiro
